{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"BillionToOne's billable test growth is slowing fast: +45% in Q1'26, +35% in Q2'26, and only +4% sequentially.","detail":"Billable tests were ~145K in Q2'25 and ~196K in Q2'26 (+35%). Six-month tests were ~275K vs ~384K (+40%). That implies Q1'25 ~130K and Q1'26 ~188K (+45%), so y/y growth fell ~10 points in one quarter. Q2'26 vs Q1'26 is 196/188 = +4% sequentially. FY23 to FY25 accessioned tests were ~272K, ~410K and ~616K (+51%, +50%). The company is a fast-growing NIPT/oncology user of Illumina-type sequencing. Test growth has slowed to about 35% even with oncology ramping.",
"url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0002070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08-05","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-Q Q2'26 and 10-K FY25 (SEC EDGAR)","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Known list has only BillionToOne +35% growth in general and prenatal revenue +55%; the quarterly test-volume deceleration and sequential +4% are not there."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"BillionToOne's cost per test has stopped falling, which suggests sequencing and reagent cost deflation is not what drives its volume.","detail":"Derived cost of revenue per accessioned test (cost of revenue / tests): FY23 $54.4M/272K = ~$200, FY24 $71.7M/410K = ~$175, FY25 $96.7M/616K = ~$157. Management-reported COGS per test was $152 in Q1'26 and $161 in Q2'26. Q2'26 is +3% y/y on oncology mix. Over six months the company calls cost per test 'relatively stable'. The decline went from -13% (FY24) to -10% (FY25) to flat. Average selling price rose +21% in Q2'26 and +25% for H1, so gross margin gains come from pricing and payor contracts, not cheaper sequencing.",
"url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070849/000207084926000017/blln-20251231.htm","date":"2026-03-11","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-K FY25 and 10-Q Q2'26","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Known list has BillionToOne cost per test only as 'relatively stable'; this adds the FY23-FY25 series, Q1/Q2'26 figures and the arithmetic."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"BillionToOne's COGS drivers are labor, supplies, phlebotomy and shipping. The company says it cuts cost per test through automation and supplier negotiations, not cheaper sequencing.","detail":"Q2'26 cost of revenue rose $9.2M y/y (to $32.3M from $23.1M, +40%). Of that, testing samples, supplies, phlebotomy and shipping added $4.9M and labor and consulting added $4.3M. That is a 53%/47% split of the rise, and none of it is a sequencing-price line. Variable costs were 56% of COGS in Q2'26 and 57% in H1'26 (54% in H1'25). Management cited 'successful negotiations with suppliers' without naming any. Sequencing cost is therefore not described as the constraint on volume.",
"url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0002070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08-05","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-Q Q2'26 MD&A","source_type":"filing","quote":"successful negotiations with suppliers","strength":"weak","why_new":"The cost-of-revenue driver breakdown has not been used; the known list cites only the cost-per-test sentence."},
{"label":"1B","direction":"supports","claim":"BillionToOne reports oncology COGS per test fell more than 10% sequentially in Q2'26, a sign that a Gb-heavy product is getting cheaper to run.","detail":"Per the Q2'26 call summary, COGS per test was $161 (vs $152 in Q1). Prenatal COGS was roughly flat sequentially and oncology COGS declined by more than 10%. The 10-Q says cost per test fell for both oncology and prenatal vs Q1'26 and Q2'25. Management aims to hold gross margin at or above 70% through pricing and further oncology cost cuts. The source does not say how much came from Illumina pricing versus workflow changes. Lower oncology cost per test, if reagent-driven, means less revenue per test for the supplier.",
"url":"https://www.marketbeat.com/instant-alerts/billiontoone-q2-earnings-call-highlights-2026-08-05/","date":"2026-08-05","source_name":"MarketBeat Q2'26 call summary (via search result)","source_type":"earnings_call","quote":"oncology cost of goods sold declined by more than 10% sequentially","strength":"weak","why_new":"Known list has no sequential oncology-vs-prenatal COGS split for BillionToOne."},
{"label":"1B","direction":"supports","claim":"BillionToOne relies on sole/single-source sequencers and reagents but keeps only about three months of inventory and says a supplier switch takes 1-9 months.","detail":"The FY25 10-K says it relies on limited or sole/single-source suppliers for certain sequencers, reagents and blood tubes. It aims to 'keep, at least, a three-month inventory' of most materials. It estimates replacing a supplier 'could take approximately one to nine months.' No Illumina purchase commitment appears in the pages read. Illumina is named only as an NIPT competitor (via Verinata), and Illumina sued BillionToOne on 7 May 2026. A customer that competes with its supplier and has no stated minimums has leverage and a reason to diversify.",
"url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070849/000207084926000017/blln-20251231.htm","date":"2026-03-11","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-K FY25 risk factors","source_type":"filing","quote":"could take approximately one to nine months","strength":"weak","why_new":"The known list has only the Illumina patent suit; the supplier-dependence language and the inventory and switching timeline are new."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"Reimbursement and payer limits, not sequencing cost, are the stated gating factors for NIPT and oncology volume at BillionToOne.","detail":"The FY25 10-K lists CMS rates of $759 for aneuploidy NIPT, ~$1,825 for the carrier PLA, ~$2,920 for Northstar Select and ~$1,644 for Northstar Response. It says overall ASP is below these code rates and out-of-network pay may be 'a small fraction of the CMS rate'. Third-party payors are more than 90% of revenue. State Medicaid coverage gaps exist (carrier screening not covered in Alabama, Michigan and Nevada; aneuploidy not covered in Nebraska or Utah). It has 250M in-network covered lives. Growth is described as coverage- and contract-driven.",
"url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070849/000207084926000017/blln-20251231.htm","date":"2026-03-11","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-K FY25","source_type":"filing","quote":"Coverage and reimbursement by third-party payors ... can be limited and uncertain.","strength":"medium","why_new":"Known list has the generic 'adoption is the binding constraint' expert view; this gives filed CMS rates, state coverage gaps and the 250M-lives count."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"NIPT penetration at BillionToOne is rising slowly while it describes the prenatal market as commoditizing.","detail":"The FY25 10-K says it tests 'approximately 1 in 11 babies'. The Q2'26 10-Q says 'more than 1 in 10', so share of US births moved from ~9.1% to above 10% in about five months. The 10-K cites 'increasing commoditization' in prenatal testing and says competitors launched recessive-condition NIPTs and RhD NIPTs in 2024. Prenatal revenue is still +55% y/y in Q2'26, but it grows mainly through ASP (+21%) and payor contracts rather than volume alone, and oncology mix adds to cost per test.",
"url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0002070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08-05","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-K FY25 and 10-Q Q2'26","source_type":"filing","quote":"we test more than 1 in 10 babies","strength":"weak","why_new":"The 1-in-11 to 1-in-10 penetration datapoint and the commoditization language are new."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"BillionToOne's oncology is ramping fast and builds Gb-heavy volume: oncology revenue is up 176% in Q2'26 and 242% in H1'26.","detail":"Oncology revenue was $13.7M in Q2'26 vs $4.9M in Q2'25 (+176%). H1'26 was $24.4M vs $7.1M (+242%). It was 11% of H1'26 revenue vs 6% a year earlier. Oncology has a higher cost per test than prenatal, which implies more sequencing per test. Management says oncology will keep growing faster than prenatal and is building a dedicated ~62,659 sq ft Union City oncology lab (June 2026 lease, opening 2027). A new 220,000 sq ft Austin site (occupancy 2027) points to more sequencing capacity. This is a watch-out for the volume-slowdown thesis.",
"url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0002070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08-05","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-Q Q2'26","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Known list has oncology +176% only as a pointer; the H1 figure, mix shift, and the Union City and Austin capacity leases are new."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"BillionToOne's capex is rising: purchases of property and equipment were $8.5M in H1'26 vs $5.2M in H1'25 (+63%).","detail":"Net PP&E was $24.9M at 30 Jun 2026 vs $20.4M at 31 Dec 2025 (+22% in six months). Non-cash capex in payables was $2.1M vs $1.4M. The 10-Q does not break out sequencers. It is still small in absolute terms: a NovaSeq X-class system costs about $1M, so this is at most a handful of instruments.",
"url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0002070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08-05","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-Q Q2'26 cash flow","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Not in the known list: the capex step-up at a growing Illumina customer."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"BillionToOne's gross-margin gain is price-led (ASP +21-25%), so a growth-rate slowdown is plausible once price gains annualize.","detail":"Gross margin was 70.4% in Q2'26 vs 65.3% in Q2'25 and 71.7% in H1'26 vs 64.9% in H1'25. FY25 was 68% vs 53% in FY24 and 24% in FY23. Overall ASP was +21% in Q2'26 and +25% in H1'26, driven by more payor contracts and the proprietary PLA code. Revenue per accessioned test was ~$264 (FY23), ~$372 (FY24) and ~$495 (FY25). Volume growth (35%) is below revenue growth (64%), so about 2/3 of growth is price/mix and not Gb.",
"url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0002070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08-05","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-K FY25 and 10-Q Q2'26","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"The price-vs-volume decomposition of BillionToOne growth is new; the known list has headline revenue growth only."}
